onsored by a Democratic firm, showed the candidates tied at 41 percent, and prediction markets give Soto roughly a one-in-three chance.\n\nSoto retains real advantages: about $1.2 million on hand to Green's $360,000, incumbency, and a favorable national climate that has drawn national Democrats to campaign in Florida. Green, a first-time nominee who won a crowded primary, has raised $1.3 million and emphasizes law-enforcement themes. Many voters remain undecided, so late advertising and turnout could decide a close race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.3442, "margin": -2.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2846, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2806, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2308, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3096, "margin": -2.6}]}